window.docContents[179].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials":{"contents":"A generalized exponential φ is specified by a recurrence and an initial\nvalue, together with strict monotonicity and continuous differentiability on\n[0,1]; its inverse ψ is called a generalized logarithm.  The first formulas\nstate these defining relations and the simplest identity choice.  Later\nformulas continue that choice across the negative and positive real axes using\nthe principal real logarithm and iterated exponentials or logarithms.\n\n\n\nThe reusable interfaces package the recurrence, normalizations, continuation\nbranches, strict monotonicity, and continuous differentiability without making\neach displayed formula assume its own conclusion.\n\n\n\n\n\n\\varphi(x+1)=\\exp(\\varphi(x)),\\qquad -1<x<\\infty.The generalized exponential satisfies this recurrence for every real x in\nthe interval (-1,∞).\n\nThe phrase “inverse generalized logarithm” is represented by a paired\ninterface: its leftInverse and rightInverse fields assert the two\ncomposition identities explicitly.\n\n\n\nThe generalized exponential and logarithm are strictly increasing on the\nclosed unit interval, and each is continuously differentiable there (indeed,\nthe interface records global ContDiff regularity).\n\n\n\n\\varphi(0)=0.The normalization fixes the generalized exponential at the origin.\n\n\\psi(e^x)=1+\\psi(x),\\qquad -\\infty<x<\\infty.The inverse generalized logarithm obeys the corresponding unit-shift relation\nfor every real x.\n\n\\psi(0)=0.The generalized logarithm has the matching origin normalization.\n\n\\varphi(x)=\\psi(x)=x,\\qquad 0\\le x\\le1.The simplest admissible choice takes both functions to be the identity on the\nunit interval.  The Lean form permits the two functions to be named while\nmaking both identity equalities explicit.\n\n\\varphi(x)=\\ln(x+1),\\qquad -1<x<0.On the interval (-1,0), the simplest generalized exponential continues as\nthe principal real logarithm of x+1.\n\n\\varphi(x)=\\underbrace{\\exp\\cdots\\exp}_{\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\ \\mathrm{times}}\n(x-\\lfloor x\\rfloor),\\qquad x>1.For x>1, let ⌊x⌋ be the natural-number floor.  Apply the ordinary\nexponential exactly that many times to the fractional part x−⌊x⌋; the\nresult is the generalized exponential.\n\n\\psi(x)=e^x-1,\\qquad -\\infty<x<0.The inverse continuation on the negative half-line is the ordinary\nexponential shifted down by one.\n\n\\psi(x)=\\ell+\\underbrace{\\ln\\cdots\\ln}_{\\ell\\ \\mathrm{times}}x,qquad x>1.For x>1, the generalized logarithm is a positive integer ℓ plus ℓ\nsuccessive principal logarithms.  The existential Lean statement records that\npositive integer rather than hiding it in an opaque choice.\n\n0\\le\\underbrace{\\ln\\cdots\\ln}_{\\ell\\ \\mathrm{times}}x<1.The positive integer ℓ is selected so that the ℓ-fold logarithm lands in\nthe half-open unit interval.  Every intermediate input to those principal\nlogarithms must remain positive; this domain guard prevents the totalized\nvalue of Real.log 0 from creating a second, spurious count.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.12 Generalized Logarithms and Exponentials","header":"§4.12 Generalized Logarithms and Exponentials","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles":{"contents":"The first subsection treats planar right triangles, with a the height\nopposite A, b the base, and c the hypotenuse.  The second treats ordinary\nplanar triangles, with a and c side lengths and b the base; all side lengths are positive and the three angles lie in\n(0, π) and sum to π, so the displayed ratios have no zero sine\ndenominators.  The final subsection uses arc lengths and angles in (0, π)\nfor a nondegenerate spherical triangle.  The accompanying figures in the\nsource fix these side/angle conventions; the formal domain predicates make\npositivity, triangle inequalities, and the spherical angle-sum condition\nexplicit.  The compatibility fields tie those measurements to the angles,\nrather than allowing an unrelated range-filtered tuple.  Degenerate and\nambiguous cases are therefore outside these exact identities rather than\nsilently totalized.\n\nFigure 4.42.1 is the source's planar right-triangle diagram.  Its labels are\na (height), b (base), and c (hypotenuse), with A the acute angle and\nC the right angle.  The formal right-triangle data uses this opposite/base/\nhypotenuse convention.\n\nFigure 4.42.2 is the source's planar-triangle diagram.  It labels a and c\nas side lengths, b as the base, and A, B, C as the corresponding\nangles.  Positivity, triangle inequalities, angle sum, and side-angle\ncompatibility are retained in the formal data.\n\nFigure 4.42.3 is the source's spherical-triangle diagram.  Lower-case a,\nb, c are arc lengths and upper-case A, B, C are angles.  The formal\ndomain restricts arcs and angles to (0, π), imposes the spherical angle-sum\ncondition, excludes the tangent poles used in E11, and records the side/angle\ncosine and sine-law compatibility.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.42 Solution of Triangles","header":"§4.42 Solution of Triangles","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___48-Software/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___48-Software--___4___48-Software--___4___48_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Introduction":{"contents":"Scientific programming languages, libraries, and systems support at least\nsome elementary functions in standard floating-point arithmetic; DLMF refers\nto §3.1(i) and does not list those routine\nimplementations here.\n\nThis page links research literature describing algorithms implemented in\nsoftware for the functions of Chapter 4 when the arithmetic is nonstandard.\nIt also includes links for the Lambert W-function and for test software.\nCitations in the bulleted lists identify papers whose research software can\nbe downloaded via the Web; references to software available in other ways are\nlisted separately.\n\nThe DLMF Software Index provides a more\ncomplete list of available software for these functions, again excluding\nimplementations using only standard floating-point arithmetic. DLMF also\npoints to GAMS class C4 as\nanother list of Web-accessible software for the functions in this chapter.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.48 Software\u0009§4.48 Software","header":"§4.48(i) Introduction","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___48-Software/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___48-Software--___4___48-Software--___4___48_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Introduction"}});